WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.990
Even a show as close to perfect as Breaking
Bad is isn't completely flawless — and we've

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.990
got the collection of mistakes to prove it.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.241
They were easy to miss, but once you notice
these errors, they're impossible to ignore.

00:00:11.241 --> 00:00:14.969
In the first shot of the pilot episode we
see Walt's khakis float gracefully through

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:17.769
the air and land in the dirt, right before
an RV runs them over.

00:00:17.769 --> 00:00:21.490
It's one of the most memorable shots of the
first season, but nothing about it makes any

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:22.490
sense at all.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.599
How did the pants land in front of the same
RV that launched them into the air in the

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:26.599
first place?

00:00:26.599 --> 00:00:27.939
Wouldn't they be flying backwards?

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:31.230
At the end of the episode, we get a different
version of the same event in which the pants

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.419
do fly backwards.

00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:36.010
So not only does the cool shot that opened
the series make no sense, but the version

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.899
we see later doesn't sync up with it.

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:41.260
Luckily, we were all too wrapped up in what
was happening to care one way or the other.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.879
In Cat's In The Bag, the second episode from
the first season, a coin flip sticks Jesse

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:48.829
with the horrible task of disposing of Emilio's
corpse.

00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:52.359
Walt tells him to buy a special bin at the
hardware store that can handle human flesh-eating

00:00:52.359 --> 00:00:53.359
acid.

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:56.370
Jesse can't find one big enough and decides
he'll just do the dirty deed in the bathroom

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:57.370
upstairs.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.649
But while Jesse's throwing the body inside
the tub, Emilio blinks!

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:04.160
Maybe he'd survived Walt's poisoning, but
if that's the case he certainly didn't survive

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.370
the acid Jesse bathed him in moments later.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.860
To be fair, neither did the bathtub - or that
entire floor of the house.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.360
In reality, though, Jesse would've been fine.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.460
Mythbusters tackled this sequence in a special
episode and proved that while enough hydrofluoric

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.479
acid could eat through flesh, it wouldn't
do nearly as much damage to ceramic and wood

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:21.780
as the show led us to believe.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.570
Still, though: please do not try this at home
or get into the meth business and have to

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.780
worry about corpse disposal to begin with.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.720
It's even less fun than the show makes it
look.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.990
In the episode entitled Over, the second season's
tenth offering, Jane Margolis rebukes Jesse

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:36.549
in front of her father, Donald.

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:40.210
Jesse's hurt by the insult and corners Jane
into having the dreaded "what are we?"

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.210
talk.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.390
During the talk Jane is snacking on a lemon
popsicle but between shots the frozen treat

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:48.600
goes from full, to half finished, to untouched
again, in the span of only a few seconds.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.189
She definitely didn't have time to wolf the
first one down and open a second and we're

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:55.840
fairly certain she didn't regurgitate the
half she'd eaten back onto the stick.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.700
Turns out she doesn't have that kind of luck
when it comes to throwing up.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.570
In the third season's episode, Green Light,
Walt temporarily retires from the meth business

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.659
in order to salvage what's left of his home
life.

00:02:05.659 --> 00:02:09.550
But Jesse recruits his idiotic friends to
continue cooking and selling Blue Sky on his

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:10.550
own.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.310
He meets up with the recently fired Walt at
the high school and shows him his product

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:17.330
in the parking lot, expecting his former teacher
to heap praise on him for its chemical purity

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:18.510
and consistency.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.080
Unfortunately, things don't go quite so smoothly.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.970
Turns out Mr. White is far too upset with
Jesse for using his signature formula without

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:29.220
permission to notice that the plastic bag
full of Blue Sky meth magically switches between

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.080
a slide-to-seal pouch and a regular zip-loc.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.730
Here's an idea, though: maybe schedule 1 narcotics
shouldn't be contained in see-through bags

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:36.730
to begin with?

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.080
In the season 3 finale, Full Measure, Mike
breaks into a small compound where the cartel

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.670
is holding Gus' chemical supplier,
Duane Chow, hostage.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:48.390
After sending balloons into the power lines,
Mike kills the two cartel enforcers who rush

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.550
outside to investigate the sudden loss of
power.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.450
If you watch closely you'll notice the glass
behind them shatters a split second before

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.450
he fires.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.170
At the end of the same sequence, Mike finds
Chow alive and alone in the back, and correctly

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:03.330
deduces from his demeanor that one last enforcer
is hiding on the other side of the wall.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:07.030
Chow raises and lowers his hands to indicate
the height at which Mike should aim his weapon

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.490
in order to shoot the enforcer.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:09.550
Mike nails it.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.390
But we have two problems here.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:14.170
One, how did Mike know exactly where the man
was standing?

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:17.730
And two, how did he manage to send a bullet
through the wall without leaving a hole?

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:22.220
In the fifth season, during the episode Madrigal,
the police talk to Peter Schuler, the German

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.580
executive of Madrigal Electromotive, about
his ties to Gus Fring.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:29.600
Schuler decides to off himself via Automated
External Defibrillator, and locks himself

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.210
in the company bathroom to do this.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:35.160
He takes off his shirt, boots up the device
and delivers a deadly shock that sends him

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.540
to the cold floor with a sickening thud.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:41.720
Problem is, even if the man had attached electrodes
properly enough for the device to detect his

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.690
heartbeat, which he didn't, the heartbeat
wasn't irregular enough to trigger the shock.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:49.780
A more realistic scene would be the police
barging into the john to find a confused,

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.740
shirtless Schuler wrestling with a device
that refuses to kill him.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.680
Walt, Mike, Jesse, Todd Alquist, and Kuby
pull off an incredible train heist in season

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:58.820
5's Dead Freight.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:02.740
They steal precious methylamine from a train
car and replace it with an equal weight of

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.610
water so the crew won't be suspicious at their
next weigh-in.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.030
This plan requires a lot of moving parts.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:11.550
Kuby had to park a sabotaged truck on the
tracks to force the train to stop.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.550
Mike would then tell Walt, Jesse and Todd
to begin the switch using massive tanks buried

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.049
in the sand beneath a bridge.

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:20.380
Here's the problem: Lydia didn't call in the
information about which train car had the

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.600
methylamine until the night before the job.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.760
But this was after they'd already selected
the bridge and buried the tanks.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.200
And Kuby couldn't have just parked the truck
off the side road intersecting the tracks

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.530
because that would have triggered the exact
robbery alarms the whole plan was designed

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:33.530
to get around.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:38.080
In the fifth season episode Gliding Over All,
Walt hires the services of white supremacist

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.190
Jack Welker and his neo-nazi gang.

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:44.000
Their job is to eliminate the imprisoned witnesses
Hank is trying to break in order to unmask

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.060
the still-at-large Heisenberg.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.360
In the absence of hazard pay, it's only a
matter of time until someone squeals.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.150
Given the potential value of the targets to
the DEA, this is a tall order.

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:57.040
But Walt goes on to insist that the targets
be killed simultaneously so as to prevent

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.630
any of them from talking later.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:04:59.630
Jack quips:

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:02.600
"Whacking bin Laden wasn't this complicated."

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:07.260
Problem is, this episode takes place in 2009,
roughly two years before the actual death

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.790
of the notorious Al Qaeda leader.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.310
Did Jack know something we didn't?

